WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.425
Detection logic depends on telemetry.

2
00:00:02.425 --> 00:00:16.313
An analytic can only evaluate behavior that the organization can observe, and its reliability depends on stable fields, understandable semantics, appropriate retention, and predictable delivery from the underlying sources.

3
00:00:16.313 --> 00:00:19.776
Detection planning should therefore begin with data requirements.

4
00:00:19.776 --> 00:00:30.322
Engineers identify the behavior to observe, determine which sources record it, confirm the necessary fields, and test how normal and suspicious scenarios appear.

5
00:00:30.322 --> 00:00:35.561
This prevents rules from being designed around data that does not exist or cannot be trusted.

6
00:00:35.561 --> 00:00:38.724
Telemetry context also affects detection quality.

7
00:00:38.724 --> 00:00:49.639
Asset criticality, identity role, device state, vulnerability information, and prior activity can help a detection distinguish routine behavior from meaningful risk.

8
00:00:49.639 --> 00:00:55.147
Poor context may create noise or leave analysts without enough information to triage an alert.

9
00:00:55.147 --> 00:00:58.190
Detection and telemetry teams need a feedback loop.

10
00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:05.344
When fields change, sources stop reporting, or analysts identify missing context, detections should be reviewed.

11
00:01:05.344 --> 00:01:12.099
Likewise, detection failures can reveal collection gaps that deserve engineering attention and coverage investment.

12
00:01:12.099 --> 00:01:15.611
Testing should cover the data pipeline as well as the analytic rule.

13
00:01:15.611 --> 00:01:27.754
Teams can generate an approved test event, confirm that the source records it, verify that collection and parsing preserve the expected fields, and then check whether the detection produces useful context.

14
00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:34.309
This end-to-end method separates logic problems from telemetry failures and supports safer change management.
